## The application's strings

`client/locales/en-US.ts` states the wording and derives the shape every other locale must match:

```ts
// client/locales/en-US.ts
import type { LocaleResource } from '@nocobase/i18n';

const enUS = {
  orders: {
    title: 'Orders',
    empty: 'No orders yet',
    count_one: '{{count}} order',
    count_other: '{{count}} orders',
  },
};

export type AppResource = LocaleResource<typeof enUS>;

export default enUS;
```

Other locales are annotated with that type, so a key you forget to translate is a compile error rather than a string that silently falls back:

```ts
// client/locales/zh-CN.ts
import type { AppResource } from './en-US.js';

const zhCN: AppResource = {
  orders: {
    title: '订单',
    empty: '暂无订单',
    count_one: '{{count}} 个订单',
    count_other: '{{count}} 个订单',
  },
};

export default zhCN;
```

**PATCH — partially update a resource when the route supports it.** This example assumes an ordinary JSON object containing changed fields; it is not a JSON Patch operation list.

```ts
const updated = await api.request<{ data: Order }>({
  path: `orders/${encodeURIComponent(orderId)}`,
  method: 'PATCH',
  json: { title: 'Updated title' },
});
```

**DELETE — remove a resource.** This example assumes the server returns `204 No Content`. If the route returns JSON, declare and consume that actual response shape instead.
